course: dictionary
lesson: 13257
title: A word for a valley
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A glen is a narrow, deep valley, often with steep sides and a stream running along the bottom. Glens are most often found in Scotland and Ireland.
explain: «We walked up the glen, with the river splashing over the stones below us.» Walked, river and splashing tell you the glen is a valley with water at the bottom. You can picture the steep sides and the stream.
explain: Glen is also a name for a person, as in «Glen is my best friend.» Here it starts with a capital letter and is doing something, so it is a name, not a valley. Ask yourself: does the sentence make sense as a place, or as a person?
fact: A glen is a narrow, deep valley, often with a stream at the bottom.
fact: Glens are often found in Scotland and Ireland.
fact: Glens were carved out of the land by rivers and glaciers over a very long time.
fact: Glen Coe is a famous glen in Scotland, and the Glens of Antrim are in Northern Ireland.
fact: Glen is also a man's name, and it starts with a capital letter.
exercise: «The narrow glen was so deep that the sun only reached the valley floor at midday.» What is a glen? | *A narrow, deep valley | A tall mountain with snow on top | A wide, flat field | A busy town street
exercise: «Glen waved to us from the top of the hill.» In this sentence, what is Glen? | A stream | A narrow, deep valley | A castle | *A person's name
exercise: Where are glens often found? | The Sahara desert | The middle of London | Australia and Japan | *Scotland and Ireland
test: «The ancient glen was carved by a river and by glaciers over thousands of years.» What carved the glen? | A bird flying over the hills | A sudden earthquake in one afternoon | Children with spades | *A river and glaciers
test: Which of these is most likely to be found at the bottom of a glen? | A railway station | *A stream | A skyscraper | A desert
exam: «The Glens of Antrim are in Ireland, and Glen Coe is in Scotland.» What do both place names have in common? | They are both cities | They both have no rivers | *They both use the word glen | They are both deserts
